<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<section xml:id="SecondDegreeFactor">
  <title>Factoring: Second Degree Expression</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Recall the distribution rules from <xref ref="Distribution">Section</xref>
      <xref ref="DistributionExample5">Example</xref>:
      <md>
        <mrow>(x+2)(x+3) \amp  = x(x+3)+2(x+3)</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp  = x^{2}+3x+2x+6</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp  = x^{2}+5x+6</mrow>
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      Notice, to arrive at our simplified expression, we took the sum of the
      <q>
        <m>x</m>
      </q>
      terms <m>3x+2x</m> to give us our middle term of <m>5x</m>.
      The constant, <m>6</m>,
      came from the multiplication of the integers from our original expression.
      Our simplified solution is a second-degree expression because it contains an <m>x^{2}</m> term.
    </p>
    <p>
      To factor an expression of the second degree,
      in the form <m>x^{2}+bx+c</m>,
      which the coefficient of the <m>x^{2}</m> term is <m>1</m>,
      we need to find numbers whose sum is the coefficient of the middle term <m>b</m> and whose product is the constant term <m>c</m>.
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <idx>
            <h>Factor</h>
            <h>Second Degree</h>
          </idx>
          <em>
            <em>Factoring Second Degree Expressions of the Form <m>x^{2}+bx+c</m>
            </em>
          </em>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                Consider all the pairs of integers whose product is <m>c</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                If the sum of any of the pairs is <m>b</m>,
                then the expression can be factored to
                <m>(x+m)(x+n)</m> where <m>m</m> and <m>n</m> are the integers.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                If none of the pairs sum to <m>b</m>,
                then the expression cannot be factored using integers.
                We then say is it
                <q>not factorable over the integers</q>.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <example xml:id="SecondDegreeFactorExample1">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor <m>x^{2}+7x+10</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>
    <p>The positive factor pairs of <m>10</m> are <m>10,1</m> (sum <m>11</m>) and <m>5,2</m> (sum <m>7</m>). The pair <m>5,2</m> has the required sum, so <m>x^{2}+7x+10=(x+5)(x+2)</m>. The factors may be written in either order.</p>
    <p>
      We can easily check our work by using the distributive law:
      <md>
        <mrow>(x+5)(x+2) \amp  = x(x)+x(2)+5(x)+5(2)</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp  = x^{2}+2x+5x+10</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp  = x^{2}+7x+10</mrow>
      </md>
    </p>
    <example xml:id="SecondDegreeFactorExample2">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor <m>x^{2}-11x+10</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>
    <p>As in <xref ref="SecondDegreeFactorExample1"/>, the positive factor pairs of <m>10</m> have sums <m>11</m> and <m>7</m>. Here the sum must be <m>-11</m>. The negative pairs are <m>-10,-1</m> (sum <m>-11</m>) and <m>-5,-2</m> (sum <m>-7</m>). Thus <m>x^{2}-11x+10=(x-10)(x-1)</m>.</p>
    <example xml:id="SecondDegreeFactorExample3">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor <m>x^{2}-7x-30</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>
    <p>
      We now need to find the factors of <m>-30</m>,
      remember that only multiplying a negative number by a positive number can give us a negative product.
      Let's examine the possibilities here:
    </p>
    <tabular>
      <row>
        <cell>Factors</cell>
        <cell>Sum</cell>
        <cell>Product</cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>
          <m>-30,1</m>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <m>-29</m>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <m>-30</m>
        </cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>
          <m>-15,2</m>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <m>-13</m>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <m>-30</m>
        </cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>
          <m>\textbf{-10,3}</m>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <m>\textbf{-7}</m>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <m>\textbf{-30}</m>
        </cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>
          <m>-6,5</m>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <m>-1</m>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <m>-30</m>
        </cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>
          <m>-5,6</m>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <m>1</m>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <m>-30</m>
        </cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>
          <m>-3,10</m>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <m>7</m>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <m>-30</m>
        </cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>
          <m>-2,15</m>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <m>13</m>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <m>-30</m>
        </cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>
          <m>-1,30</m>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <m>29</m>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <m>-30</m>
        </cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
    <p>
      The factors of <m>-10</m> and <m>3</m> give us the sum and product that we are looking for.
      Therefore, we can factor <m>x^{2}-7x-30</m> into <m>(x-10)(x+3)</m>. }
    </p>
    <example xml:id="SecondDegreeFactorExample4">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor <m>2x^{2}+16x+30</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>
    <p>
      It is important to recognize here that the coefficient of the <m>x^{2}</m> terms here does not equal <m>1</m>.
      We can however, begin by dividing by a common factor,
      which in this case is <m>2</m> and once we do that we are left with:
      <me>
        2x^{2}+16x+30=2(x^{2}+8x+15)
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>To factor completely, factor <m>x^{2}+8x+15</m>. The pairs <m>15,1</m> and <m>5,3</m> have sums <m>16</m> and <m>8</m>, respectively. Hence <m>2x^{2}+16x+30=2(x+5)(x+3)</m>.</p>
    <example xml:id="SecondDegreeFactorExample5">
      <statement>
        <p>Factor <m>x^{3}+x^{2}-6x</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </example>
    <p>This third-degree expression has common factor <m>x</m>, so <m>x^{3}+x^{2}-6x=x(x^{2}+x-6)</m>. To factor the quadratic, use integers whose product is <m>-6</m> and sum is <m>1</m>. The pair is <m>3,-2</m>, giving <m>x(x+3)(x-2)</m>.</p>
    <example xml:id="SecondDegreeFactorExample6">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor: <m>x^{2}-25</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>
    <p>Write <m>x^{2}-25=x^{2}+0x-25</m>. We need integers with product <m>-25</m> and sum <m>0</m>: <m>5,-5</m>. Thus <m>x^{2}-25=(x+5)(x-5)</m>. Distribution checks the result: <m>x^{2}-5x+5x-25=x^{2}-25</m>.</p>
    <p>The middle terms cancel because the integers have opposite signs. This is the <term>difference of squares</term> pattern: <m>a^{2}-b^{2}=(a+b)(a-b)</m>.</p>
    <example xml:id="SecondDegreeFactorExample7">
      <statement>
        <p>Factor <m>t^{2}-36</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </example>
    <p>Since <m>36=6^{2}</m>, <m>t^{2}-36=(t+6)(t-6)</m>.</p>
    <example xml:id="SecondDegreeFactorExample8">
      <statement>
        <p>Factor <m>2w^{2}-32</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </example>
    <p>First factor out <m>2</m>, then use the difference of squares: <m>2w^{2}-32=2(w^{2}-16)=2(w+4)(w-4)</m>.</p>
  </introduction>
  <exercises>
    <title>Exercises</title>
    <exercisegroup cols="2">
      <title>Factoring quadratic trinomials</title>
      <introduction>
        <p>Factor each expression completely.</p>
      </introduction>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>x^{2}+5x+6</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            <m>(x+2)(x+3)</m>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>a^{2}-a-6</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>g^{2}+g-6</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            <m>(g+3)(g-2)</m>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>r^{2}-4r-5</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>x^{2}+9x+14</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            <m>(x+7)(x+2)</m>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>x^{2}-12x+36</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>x^{2}+9x+18</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            <m>(x+3)(x+6)</m>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>y^{2}+7y-30</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>t^{2}+9t+20</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            <m>(t+5)(t+4)</m>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>g^{2}-13g+36</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>x^{2}+10x-24</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            <m>(x+12)(x-2)</m>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>h^{2}+h-30</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>f^{2}-7f+12</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            <m>(f-3)(f-4)</m>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>v^{2}+14v+48</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>r^{2}-10r-24</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            <m>(r-12)(r+2)</m>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>p^{2}-26p+48</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    </exercisegroup>
    <exercisegroup cols="2">
      <title>Common factors and trinomials</title>
      <introduction>
        <p>Factor each expression completely.</p>
      </introduction>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>2b^{2}+10b+12</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            <m>2(b+3)(b+2)</m>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>2w^{2}+12w+10</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>5x^{2}-50x+125</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            <m>5(x-5)(x-5)</m>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>x^{3}+3x^{2}-10x</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>2w^{3}-4w^{2}+2w</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            <m>2w(w-1)(w-1)</m>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </exercisegroup>
    <exercisegroup cols="2">
      <title>Differences of squares</title>
      <introduction>
        <p>Factor each expression completely.</p>
      </introduction>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>x^{2}-100</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>a^{2}-49</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            <m>(a+7)(a-7)</m>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>3x^{2}-75</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>4x^{2}-36</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            <m>4(x-3)(x+3)</m>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>q^{2}-225</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>2p^{2}-72</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            <m>2(p+6)(p-6)</m>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>r^{2}-121</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    </exercisegroup>
  </exercises>
</section>
